  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">TAF-  &apos;We Live to Shine&apos; Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Under their previous empowerment grant with Voice, The Albino Foundation (TAF) focused on building the self-esteem of members of the albino community and creating awareness on issues affecting the community. Through the community engagements that happened under the project, they raised awareness, built synergies and networks. Current project would like to leverage on these synergies and networks to deepen awareness on issues affecting the community, enhance access to social services for the community andstrengthen the capacity of members to engage in political spaces and make their voices heard.

Key activities planned under current project are:

&#x2022; Strengthen the capacity of persons with albinism (PWA) on their rights through workshops.
&#x2022; Advocacy engagement to duty bearers and other relevant stakeholders in the state on access to social services, such as education, health, and political participation of persons with albinism in electoral process.
&#x2022; Sensitization of headteachers on how to ensure access to quality education for PWA.
&#x2022; Sensitization workshop for primary healthcare providers on how to support PWA in accessing healthcare services especially where it concerns skin cancer and refractive errors.
&#x2022; Celebrate international albinism day byorganizing a road walk, talent, and fashion show to showcase the different abilities and beauty of PWA&#x2019;s.
&#x2022; Weekly radio program to further create awareness on issues and challenges around albinism


The change they would like to see is a society where persons with albinism are bold enough to come out and take theirrightful places in their homes, are more visible in the society and public places, vote and be voted for during elections; are ableto demand for their rights and their rights are protected and enforced where necessary. They desire a society where more persons with albinism have unhindered access to quality education &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; health services, especially those in the rural areas.</narrative>
  </description>
